That's a clean design — essentially a **shepherd init**. The logic maps directly to kernel syscalls. Here's exactly what you need:

---

## Core Syscalls You'll Use

| What | Syscall | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Launch program | `fork()` + `execve()` | Spawn a child process |
| Detect failure | `waitpid(-1, &status, 0)` | Block until any child exits, get exit code |
| Force kill all | `kill(-1, SIGKILL)` | Sends SIGKILL to every process except PID 1 |
| Catch crashes | `sigaction(SIGSEGV/SIGABRT...)` | Intercept crash signals |
| Mount early fs | `mount()` | Get `/proc` `/sys` `/dev` up before anything |

---

## Your Logic as PID 1 Pseudocode

```c
main() {
    mount_essential_fs();       // /proc, /sys, /dev

    shell = "/bin/sh";          // or your default program

    while (1) {
        pid = launch(shell);

        wait_for(pid);          // blocks until shell exits

        // shell exited/crashed — try 3 times
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            pid = launch(shell);
            if (wait_for(pid) == SUCCESS) break;
        }
        // after 3 failures, loop restarts — always comes back here
    }
}
```

For **launching other programs** with retry logic:

```c
launch_with_retry(program) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        pid = fork();
        if (pid == 0) execve(program, args, env);   // child

        status = waitpid(pid, ...);
        if (WIFEXITED(status) && WEXITSTATUS(status) == 0)
            return SUCCESS;
    }
    return FAIL;   // caller falls back to shell
}
```

For **"system is crashing, dump everything"**:

```c
void panic_handler(int sig) {
    kill(-1, SIGKILL);    // kills ALL processes except PID 1 instantly
    // optionally write a crash log to /dev/kmsg or a file
    launch(shell);        // bring shell back up
}
```

Register that for `SIGSEGV`, `SIGABRT`, `SIGBUS` — the signals that mean something went seriously wrong.

---

## The "API to Launch Programs" Part

Since you're on bare kernel with no D-Bus or anything, the simplest API is a **Unix domain socket** or a **named FIFO**:

```
/run/init.sock   ← your process manager listens here
```

Any process sends a message like `launch:/bin/myapp` and your manager forks it, applies the 3-retry rule, and sends back the PID or an error. That's it.

---

## The One PID 1 Rule the Kernel Enforces

You already know this intuitively — **the outer `while(1)` loop must never exit**. If `main()` returns, kernel panics. Your design already handles this correctly since the loop always falls back to launching the shell.

Also: **reap all orphans**, not just your direct children. Any process whose parent dies gets reparented to PID 1. Add a `SIGCHLD` handler that calls `waitpid(-1, WNOHANG)` in a loop to drain the zombie queue — otherwise you'll slowly accumulate zombie entries in the process table.

---

## Files to Create in Your Initramfs

```
/init          ← your process manager (statically compiled, this IS PID 1)
/bin/sh        ← busybox sh or any static shell binary
/dev/          ← empty dir, your init mounts devtmpfs here
/proc/         ← empty dir, mount procfs here
/sys/          ← empty dir, mount sysfs here
/run/          ← where your socket lives
```

Compile your init statically (`gcc -static`) so it has zero library dependencies. That's the only hard requirement.

Want me to look at any specific part of the kernel source that's relevant to how PID 1 interacts with the kernel — signal delivery, zombie reaping, or the mount calls?

